      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The number of endothelial progenitor cells (EPCs) descends when atherosclerosis developed. The objective was to compare the number of CD34(+)CD133(+) cells with the severity of atherosclerosis assessed by Syntax score."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study included 80 patients with stable angina undergoing coronary angiography. Patients were classified into single-vessel group, multiple-vessel group and normal group according to angiography. The percentage of CD34(+)CD133(+) cells in the mononuclear cells isolated from peripheral blood of different groups by flow cytometric analysis was compared. The quantity of CD34(+)CD133(+) EPCs was log transformed to improve normality (lgEPC). Syntax score was used in this study to assess the extent of coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The level of lgEPC was lower in the single-vessel group than that in the normal group (-3.42 ± 0.44 versus -3.17 ± 0.39, P < 0.05), and the level of lgEPC was lower in the multiple-vessel group than that in the single vessel group (-3.63 ± 0.31 versus -3.42 ± 0.44, P < 0.05). An inverse correlation between lgEPC and Syntax score analyzed by linear regression."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "EPC level probably serves as a predictor of the development and severity of atherosclerosis on a cellular level. EPC, a relatively more important risk factor, perhaps protects against coronary artery disease."
        }
      ]},
